  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Monday)\nIssued at 1205 PM CDT TUE SEP 22 2026\n\nA weakening frontal boundary/convergence zone will be in place\nacross Central AL today, leading to at least some focus for\nafternoon thunderstorm development. This is generally the impetus\nfor the Marginal (Level 1 of 5) Risk for severe storms today.\nOverall coverage of thunderstorms will be low with only a 20-30%\nchance across the area, but given the dry air aloft and the\nwarm/humid surface, any of the storms may produce damaging wind\ngusts.\n\nWe start to see a deepening trough to our east Wednesday through\nthe weekend with ridging building to our west. This will lead to\nmore northeasterly to easterly flow for Central AL for the second\nhalf of the week leading to cooler and drier weather through the\nweekend. Compared to the past few weeks of above normal\ntemperatures, this will be a welcome start to the Fall season\nwith highs in the low to mid 80s for much of the area (a few\nspots in the upper 70s in the far eastern portions of Central AL)\nand lows in the mid 50s to low 60s. This pattern continues\nthrough at least Monday with limited to no chance for rain\nThursday through Monday.\n\n25/Owen",
